{
  "version": 1,
  "notes": "Shared color test vectors (Spec 76 §1.6). Consumed by hypercolor-color (Rust, include_str!) and the SDK color module (Bun test); drift on either side fails CI. TOLERANCE: byte outputs compare exactly; float outputs compare within `tol` (absolute, per component), falling back to default_tolerance when a vector omits it. Hue components compare by shortest angular distance. `expected: null` means the parse MUST fail. `oklab_roundtrip` expectations are the input itself: the assertion is that the round trip is an identity within `tol`. TIE RULE: the documented cross-language bound is +/-1 LSB at exact rounding ties, because Rust computes in f32 and TypeScript in f64. Byte-output vectors therefore exclude any input whose true channel value lands within 1e-4 of an exact .5 byte boundary; the generator verifies that computationally against an f64 mirror rather than by inspection. Rounding ties are covered by the Rust-side palette oracle and by each language's own unit tests, never here. Adding a whole-degree saturated hue at value 0.5, or a scale factor that halves an odd byte, will reintroduce a tie: regenerate instead of hand-editing. PROVENANCE: every expected value is produced by running the real hypercolor-color kernel (wave 1.5), and every float tolerance is the smallest decade leaving 4x headroom over the measured f32-vs-f64 divergence for that vector. Hand-computed expectations are not accepted. Every oklab_from_linear expectation is additionally cross-checked against the palette crate's f64 Oklab conversion (the same oracle oracle_tests.rs measures against), which covers the published white, primary and secondary references; the f32 kernel agrees with it to within 1e-7 on all of them.",
